NNAAMMEE
     vvnndd — vnode disk driver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppsseeuuddoo--ddeevviiccee vvnndd [_c_o_u_n_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The vvnndd driver provides a disk-like interface to a file.  This is useful
     for a variety of applications, such as building miniroot or floppy disk
     images.

     This document assumes familiarity with how to generate kernels and how to
     properly configure disks and pseudo-devices in a kernel configuration
     file.

     In order to compile in support for vvnndd devices, a line similar to the
     following must be present in the kernel configuration file:

           pseudo-device  vnd  4    # vnode disk driver

     The _c_o_u_n_t argument is how many vvnnddss memory is allocated for at boot time.
     In this example, no more than 4 vvnnddss may be configured.

     There is a run-time utility that is used for configuring vvnnddss.  See
     vnconfig(8) for more information.

IIOOCCTTLL IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE
     The following ioctl(2) calls are defined in <_d_e_v_/_v_n_d_i_o_c_t_l_._h>:

     VNDIOCSET _s_t_r_u_c_t _v_n_d___i_o_c_t_l _*
             Associate the file _v_n_d___f_i_l_e with a vvnndd pseudo device, optionally
             encrypted using the Blowfish cipher and the key specified in
             _v_n_d___k_e_y of length _v_n_d___k_e_y_l_e_n.  The size of the configured device
             is returned in _v_n_d___s_i_z_e.

                   struct vnd_ioctl {
                           char    *vnd_file;
                           off_t   vnd_size;
                           u_char  *vnd_key;
                           int     vnd_keylen;
                   };

     VNDIOCCLR _s_t_r_u_c_t _v_n_d___i_o_c_t_l _*
             Disassociate a vvnndd device.

     VNDIOCGET _s_t_r_u_c_t _v_n_d___u_s_e_r _*
             Get the associated file name, device, inode number, and unit num‐
             ber of a vvnndd device.  If _v_n_u___u_n_i_t is -1, information on the vvnndd
             device corresponding to the file descriptor passed to ioctl(2)
             will be returned.  Otherwise, _v_n_u___u_n_i_t may contain the unit num‐
             ber of another vvnndd device.  This allows for opening just vnd0 and
             querying all available devices.

                   struct vnd_user {
                           char    vnufile[VNDNLEN];
                           int     vnu_unit;
                           dev_t   vnu_dev;
                           ino_t   vnu_ino;
                   };

     Additionally, some disklabel(8) related ioctl(2) calls defined in
     <_s_y_s_/_d_k_i_o_._h> are available: DIOCGDINFO, DIOCSDINFO, and DIOCWDINFO.  They
     are documented in sd(4).

